@government
title: How Government Works

= How Government Works

The campaign keeps the Crown, cabinet, Majles, and Senate distinct. The Shah's
relationship, resistance, court capacity, and electoral influence are separate
qualities. Majles returns are not usable until the corresponding scenario
credential and usability states permit it.

Research map:
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-maj-s3">!}MAJ-S3{!</a>!}, chapter 3;
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-sup-007">!}SUP-007{!</a>!}, Sixteenth Majles
proceedings; and
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-sup-051">!}SUP-051{!</a>!}. See the generated
{!<a href="timeline.html">!}historical primer{!</a>!}.

- @research_library: Library index

@timeline
title: Timeline

= Timeline

* February 1949: attempt on the Shah, martial law, and political restriction.
* 1949: constituent assembly and preparation of the first Senate.
* October–November 1949: palace protest and National Front formation.
* February 1950: Sixteenth Majles and First Senate convene.
* December 1950: supplemental agreement rejected.
* 7–20 March 1951: Razmara's assassination and parliamentary approval of the
  nationalization principle.

Research map:
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-maj-s1">!}MAJ-S1{!</a>!}, chapter 15;
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-maj-s3">!}MAJ-S3{!</a>!}, chapter 3; and
{!<a href="timeline.html#source-sup-006">!}SUP-006{!</a>!}, pp. 15–16.

